  "query_preview": "..\nToday through Thursday: A high pressure center off the \nCalifornia coast will support a ridge building along the Pacific\ncoastline into the Gulf of Alaska. A few lingering showers over\neastern Washington and the Idaho Panhandle will dissipate \nheading into Wednesday afternoon as the earlier low exits the \nregion. The gradient is slow to loosen and keeps wind in the \nteens through the day. Ensembles have slowed on how fast the \nridge builds. It has trended the highs down from previous \nforecast. It could be due to the strong low over Manitoba and a\nsupporting trough stretching over BC. But minor HeatRisk is \nstill the main concern through the end of the week. Highs for \ntoday will be in the 60s and low 70s and 70s and low 80s for the\nrest of the week. Overnight lows will be in the upper 30s and \n40s.\n\nFriday through Tuesday: By Friday morning, the trough drops \ninto the US. There is poor agreement on the ensembles regarding \nthe track. Currently it is expected to clip the North Idaho \nPanhandle and Northeast WA. The trough could generate light \nprecip and isolated thunderstorms. Friday is the best chance of\nany precip through the start of next week. The north to south \nflow pattern with the trough will bring gusty winds of 25 to 30 \nmph down the Okanogan Valley late Friday night into Saturday \nmorning. The ridge axis will move over Washington Sunday through\nTuesday and continue the dry, warming trend. Minor to Moderate \nHeatRisk will be widespread across the Inland Northwest. By \nMonday, highs are expected to be in the 80 and 90s. Lows will be\nin the 50s. /JDC",
